{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high vs. outcomes (often by ~2–6 €/t), especially during downtrends.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the recent history rolls over, the model follows too slowly and keeps prices elevated for ~1–2 weeks; apply a faster negative drift when the last 5–10 days momentum is negative.",
    "Missed drawdowns: the model underestimates the depth of sell-offs (true series falls further and faster); increase downside sensitivity when volatility rises and recent lows are breaking.",
    "False rebound / overshoot: model frequently predicts rebounds back toward mid-70s/upper-70s that do not materialize; dampen mean-reversion strength after a regime shift lower.",
    "Horizon effect: errors grow with lead time—near-term is closer, but weeks 2–4 are materially too high; apply a horizon-dependent downward adjustment (larger haircut beyond day ~10).",
    "Level anchoring to prior regime: forecasts appear anchored to the earlier 78–83 range even after the market transitions to high-60s; introduce a regime-detection rule (e.g., if 10–15d MA breaks below 20–30d MA, shift forecast baseline down)."
  ]
}
